# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""The privacy crosswalk rows. Hand-written. Edit here, then run build_privacy_crosswalk.py.

One row is one GDPR obligation an organisation has to meet.

  gdpr        (article number, label shown on the card). Titles and EUR-Lex anchors come
              from gdpr.json.
  sel         the provisions of the row in the notation of the Data Protection Mapping
              Project ("5.1.a" is Article 5(1)(a)). "28.3" takes the provision and everything
              under it, "=28.3" the provision alone. The ISO column is the union of the
              ISO/IEC 27701:2019 clauses that project pairs with these provisions: it is
              computed, never typed in.
  obligation  this site's paraphrase, checked against the Regulation.
  nist        NIST Privacy Framework 1.0 subcategories: this site's own reading of the
              GDPR provisions and of the subcategory text. A subcategory is listed only
              where achieving its outcome would implement or evidence part of the
              obligation; preconditions and general governance outcomes are left out unless
              the row is about them. The builder adds one mark, "agrees with the NIST-hosted
              GDPR crosswalk", where that crosswalk pairs the same subcategory with a
              provision of the row (nist_hosted_support.json). It is a cross-check only.
  gap         how far the Privacy Framework goes, in this site's reading: "" (addresses
              the topic), "partial" or "none". The ISO side carries no such judgement,
              because the standard's text was not read.
  note        what the frameworks leave to the law. About ISO/IEC 27701 it states only
              what the published mapping lists and what the clause titles say.
  tool        a related tool on this site, (href, label), or None.

British spelling. No em dashes.
"""

